#
# prima-materia — Hasidism
#
# Opened from the structure-driven entrance of SPEC §14: registered at
# pm:corpusNamed in traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl with no node. What was
# opened is Tzava'at ha-Rivash, the earliest printed manual of Hasidic conduct,
# read complete in the Hebrew, all 143 sections, with two English translations
# by two translators compared at every passage entered. The three works the
# registration named stay unopened and are declared below; the work read is a
# selection from the same body of teaching as the second of them.
#
# Order of the file: the works; the tradition with the three decisions; the
# practices and the capacities the work says they bring about; what must come
# first; the cautions, several with a bodily consequence; what the work says a
# failed service looks like; the signs; and one place where the work reads its
# own failure as a mark of nearness.
#
# Citation. Every node cites the Hebrew by section, in the Warsaw printing of
# 1913, and two English translations by section: Jacob Immanuel Schochet's of
# 1998, with the page on which the section begins, and the adaptation of Amiram
# and Yehudah Shimon Markel of 2020, which has no pages. Schochet translates
# from his own Hebrew edition, which is not the Warsaw text, and where the two
# differ the node says so.
#

#
# The works
#

pmw:TzavaatHaRivash a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, the Testament of Rabbi Israel Baal Shem"@en, "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, das Testament des Rabbi Israel Baal Schem"@de ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-15 in the Hebrew, sections 1 to 143, in the printing Warsaw 1913 as given in the Sefaria library from the scan of the National Library of Israel (Rosetta IE13133653), public domain. The square brackets of that printing are kept in the citations where they matter: they mark matter the printer added from parallel collections, and sections 31, 43, 90, 101 and 143 carry such brackets."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations by two translators. Jacob Immanuel Schochet, Tzava'at Harivash: The Testament of Rabbi Israel Baal Shem Tov, English translation with introduction, notes and commentary (Brooklyn: Kehot Publication Society 1998), the title page in the full text; access archive.org, tsavaat-harivash-testament-of-rabbi-israel-baal-shem-tov-jacob-immanuel-schochet, with a clean English text layer. Schochet follows the section division of his own Hebrew edition and joins some sections (2-3, 5-6, 7-8, 13-14, 17-19 and others) without renumbering. Amiram Markel and Yehudah Shimon Markel, The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, adapted into English (2020), as given in the Sefaria library under CC-BY; it calls itself an adaptation and was read as a translation only where its wording stays with the Hebrew. A third English version in the same library, of the YU Torah miTzion Beit Midrash, covers three sections and was not used."@en ;
    skos:note "What the work is, and it decides how any node in this file may be counted. The title page ascribes the instructions to the Baal Shem Tov and adds rules of upright conduct from Dov Ber of Mezhirech; it calls itself found in the valise of Isaiah, head of the court of Yanov. The first edition is undated, 1792 or 1793. Schochet's introduction (pp. xi-xvi) finds every section of it, all but six, in other anthologies of the Maggid of Mezhirech's teaching — seventy-four in Likkutim Yekarim, forty-three in the Likkutei Amarim printed under the name of Menachem Mendel of Vitebsk, thirty-three in Or Torah, and others in Maggid Devarav le-Ya'akov and Or ha-Emet — and concludes that it is an edited selection from one body of manuscripts of the Maggid's circle, made from defective copies; the text names the Baal Shem Tov at nineteen sayings and the Maggid at one. He quotes Shneur Zalman of Liadi, a disciple of the Maggid, that the book is not at all the Baal Shem Tov's last will and that he decreed nothing before his death, but that its contents are gleanings of his sayings. The nodes below therefore attest the teaching of the Maggid's circle as printed about 1792, and a node says 'the Baal Shem Tov' only where the section names him."@en ;
    skos:note "The opposition the work met is reported but not read. Schochet (p. xv, note 18) says that the anti-Hasidic tract Zemir Aritzim ve-Charvot Tzurim, printed in 1772 in the Maggid's lifetime, attacks the Hasidic emphasis on joy against melancholy in wording that shows its author had a manuscript containing section 46. The tract was not opened in this run; Schochet cites it in M. Wilensky's edition, which is not public domain."@en .

pmw:ToledotYaakovYosef a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Toledot Ya'akov Yosef of Jacob Joseph of Polonne"@en, "Toledot Ja'akow Josef des Jakob Josef von Polonne"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Reachable: the Sefaria library answered on 2026-10-15 with an index of the work, title page, introduction and the weekly portions. The earliest printed Hasidic book, 1780, and the one that cites the Baal Shem Tov by name most often."@en .

pmw:KeterShemTov a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Keter Shem Tov, the anthology of the Baal Shem Tov's sayings compiled by Aaron of Opatów"@en, "Keter Schem Tow, die Sammlung der Aussprüche des Baal Schem Tow"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not harvested in this run. Reachable: the Sefaria library carries it in the printing Zolkiew 1794-95 in two parts. One passage of it was read at its place, part 1, section 184 of that printing, which Schochet cites as section 216 of the Kehot edition, because it bears on section 63 of Tzava'at ha-Rivash; it is used in the examination at examinations/the-one-who-stays-outside-tested.ttl and is not entered as a node of this file, since the work has not been opened for harvest."@en .

pmw:Tanya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Likkutei Amarim, Tanya, of Shneur Zalman of Liadi"@en, "Likkutei Amarim, Tanja, des Schneur Salman von Ljadi"@de ;
    skos:note "Named by the registration and not opened in this run. Reachable: the Sefaria library answered on 2026-10-15 with an index of the work in its five parts."@en .

#
# The tradition
#

pmt:Hasidism a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Hasidism"@en, "Chassidismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The eastern European transmission of devekut as a continuous attachment attainable within ordinary life, and of contemplation as its means."@en ;
    skos:note "Contact route, the first of the three decisions SPEC §14 requires, kept at documented, and the route is in the text. Tzava'at ha-Rivash cites the Zohar and the Tikkunei Zohar by name, the Lurianic unifications as known from the Ari (sections 82 and 90), the Talmud and the Mishnah throughout; its seven traits are the lower sefirot and its talk of shells and sparks is the Lurianic vocabulary. Any agreement between this file and the Kabbalah, Zohar or Lurianic material of this holding is reception along that route and counts once."@en ;
    skos:note "Recension and edition, the second decision. The Warsaw printing of 1913 is read as the text because it is the one reachable in full in the Hebrew. It is not Schochet's text, and the difference shows at section 31, where a saying of the Baal Shem Tov on the little holy spirit stands in the Warsaw printing in square brackets and is absent from Schochet's text, who gives it from Likkutim Yekarim in a note, and at section 14, where the Warsaw printing has six times the seven nations and both translations have the six nations."@en ;
    skos:note "The third decision, the number of citable places: 143 sections, of which two are doublets (57 of 42, 113 of 51 by Schochet's count). The nodes below cite forty-odd of them."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading found that a reader would not expect from the registration's one-line definition. The work is a manual of attachment, and most of it is about the mind; but it carries a set of plain bodily claims — that guarding a commandment all day prevents nocturnal emission, that looking at a beautiful woman for desire causes it, that intense prayer could by nature kill, that fasting may harm the eyes, that a sick body weakens prayer, that seclusion alone is dangerous — and several recipes that promise a desire will leave on a recited formula. Against these stand more failure conditions than signs: the work says whose service fails and why five times and names three signs of success, and one of the failure conditions is a criterion a reader could apply to himself, that a service which comes and goes was never proper."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Toledot Yaakov Yosef; Keter Shem Tov; Tzava'at ha-Rivash; Shneur Zalman, Tanya" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash, pmw:ToledotYaakovYosef, pmw:KeterShemTov, pmw:Tanya ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 82 and 90, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" .

#
# Attachment, its practice and what it is said to bring
#

pmp:HasidismHitdabkut a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Attaching one's thought to Above at all times"@en, "Das Denken jederzeit nach oben heften"@de ;
    skos:definition "The practice the work turns on, given in many forms: to attach one's thought above and not look at the matters of this world (5); to think oneself one of the upper world (6); to be secluded in thought with the Shekhinah, saying always, when shall I merit that its light dwell with me (8); to have one thought only in the service, and when speaking of worldly matters to think of it as going out of one's house meaning to return at once (84); to attach oneself and then ask for some household need or do some worldly thing, only to accustom oneself to remain attached while doing it (81)."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 5-8, 24, 81 and 84, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash: The Testament of Rabbi Israel Baal Shem Tov, trans. Jacob Immanuel Schochet (Brooklyn: Kehot 1998), sects. 5-8, 24, 81 and 84, pp. 6-7, 20, 68-70" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, adapted into English by Amiram Markel and Yehudah Shimon Markel (2020), sects. 5-8, 24, 81 and 84" .

pmc:HasidismHishtavut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Equanimity: praise and shame, delicacies and plain food, alike"@en, "Gleichmut: Lob und Schmach, Leckerbissen und schlichte Speise gleich"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state the work calls a very great level (2) and a great rule (10): that it is all the same to a man whether people praise or shame him, whether they hold him ignorant or learned in the whole Torah, whether he eats delicacies or other things, because the evil inclination has been entirely removed from him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 2 and 10, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 2-3 and 10, pp. 4 and 9" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 2 and 10" .

pmc:HasidismDevekutHishtavut a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Constant attachment brings equanimity"@en, "Beständiges Anhaften bringt Gleichmut"@de ;
    skos:definition "Section 10, which the Hebrew marks as a saying of the Baal Shem Tov's school: what causes the equanimity is attachment to the Creator at all times, because, busy with attaching himself, he has no leisure to think of such things."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HasidismHitdabkut ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HasidismHishtavut ;
    skos:note "The mechanism the work gives is occupation and not transformation: the attached man is not said to have stopped caring about praise, but to have had no room to attend to it. A third party could check equanimity only by its outward side — whether the man answers an insult — and section 49 forbids answering even with good words, so that the outward sign is prescribed as a rule and cannot afterwards be read as evidence of the state."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 10 and 49, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 10 and 49, pp. 9 and 42" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 10 and 49" .

pmc:HasidismRuachHakodeshMeat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "A little holy spirit: the thought that comes in attachment is likely so; and knowing what to do from the day's study"@en, "Ein wenig heiliger Geist: der Gedanke, der im Anhaften kommt, trifft wahrscheinlich zu; und aus dem Tagesstudium wissen, was zu tun ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two capacities in section 31. The first, a saying of the Baal Shem Tov: when one is attached to the Creator and a thought about some matter falls into one's mind, it is probably so as it fell into the thought, and this is a little holy spirit, ruach ha-kodesh me'at. The second: when something comes one's way and one does not know whether to do it, one can understand from the matter one studied that day how to act."@en ;
    skos:note "The first capacity stands in the Warsaw printing inside square brackets and is not in Schochet's text; he gives it in his note 2 to section 31 (pp. 24-25) as the opening of the same passage in Likkutim Yekarim, sections 11-12. It is entered because it is in the text read, and it attests that collection as much as this one. Markel translates the bracketed text with the rest."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 31, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sect. 31 with note 2, pp. 24-25" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 31" .

pmc:HasidismDevekutRuachHakodesh a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Attachment yields the little holy spirit and the guidance from the day's study"@en, "Anhaften bringt den kleinen heiligen Geist und die Weisung aus dem Tagesstudium"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition is stated twice: the thought is likely true when it falls while one is attached; and the guidance from the day's study is assured only if one is always attached to God, for then God will always arrange that one knows from the Torah."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HasidismHitdabkut ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HasidismRuachHakodeshMeat ;
    skos:note "The failing case is in the same section, and it is stated as a measure for measure: one who walks with God by chance, be-keri, God walks with by chance, and does not arrange for him the garments and foods that carry the sparks belonging to the root of his soul. Read as a claim, the first capacity is the most checkable thing in the work: a thought about a matter, had in a stated state, said to be probably right. The word is probably, mistama, and the work names no count and no case."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 31, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sect. 31, pp. 24-25" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 31" .

pmp:HasidismHitbodedut a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Seclusion with the Creator"@en, "Abgeschiedenheit mit dem Schöpfer"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hitbodedut, to seclude oneself with the Creator, given as a means to attachment together with writing down the secrets of the Torah and performing the unifications known from the Ari, thinking during them of God's greatness as far as one can (82). The work allows it in three settings: two people in one room, each secluded on his own (63); in a house full of people, when one is already attached (63); and lying in bed, seeming to people to be asleep (133)."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 63, 82 and 133, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 63, 82-83 and 133, pp. 50, 68 and 130" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 63, 82 and 133" .

pmc:HasidismDevekut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Devekut, attachment to the Creator, as a state attained"@en, "Dewekut, das Anhaften am Schöpfer, als erlangter Zustand"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state the practices aim at: the thought bound continuously to God and the upper world, kept while studying, speaking and doing worldly things. The work grades it into smallness, katnut, in which one only thinks that the whole earth is full of His glory and He is near, and greatness, gadlut, in which one rises in thought through all the firmaments above the angels, ofanim, seraphim and thrones (67, 129, 137)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 29-30, 67, 129 and 137, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 29-30, 67, 129 and 137, pp. 21-23, 53, 123 and 132" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 29-30, 67, 129 and 137" .

pmc:HasidismHitbodedutDevekut a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Seclusion, writing down the secrets and the unifications bring attachment"@en, "Abgeschiedenheit, Niederschrift der Geheimnisse und die Vereinigungen bringen das Anhaften"@de ;
    skos:definition "Section 82: one will merit attachment through seclusion from people, through writing secrets of the Torah, and through the unifications as known from the Ari."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HasidismHitbodedut ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HasidismDevekut ;
    skos:note "The same practice carries, in section 63, the only warning in the work that names danger to the practitioner in that word, entered at pmc:HasidismHitbodedutYechidiSakana."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 82, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 82-83, p. 68" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 82" .

#
# Prayer and what it is said to bring
#

pmp:HasidismTefilaBeKavanah a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Prayer with great intention, word by word"@en, "Gebet mit großer Absicht, Wort für Wort"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prayer as the work prescribes it: from degree to degree, beginning slowly and reaching great attachment in the middle (32); in a low voice, crying out in a whisper with all one's strength (33); putting one's whole strength into every word, since each word is a complete figure (34); prolonging the word one cannot bear to leave (70); saying the word in great connection rather than with the known Lurianic intentions, so that all intentions are included of themselves (118); at times with no motion at all (105)."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 32-34, 70, 105 and 118, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 32-34, 70, 105 and 118, pp. 25-26, 56, 97 and 108" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 32-34, 70, 105 and 118" .

pmc:HasidismGiluiElyonim a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Revelation of supernal matters"@en, "Offenbarung der oberen Dinge"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity section 41 ascribes to the Rav, whom Schochet identifies with the Baal Shem Tov: that higher things were revealed to him, nitgalu elav devarim elyonim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 41, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sect. 41, p. 28" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 41" .

pmc:HasidismTefilaGiluiElyonim a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Prayer always with great intention, and not much learning, brought the revelations"@en, "Gebet stets mit großer Absicht, nicht viel Gelehrsamkeit, brachte die Offenbarungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Section 41: the soul said to the Rav that he merited the revelation of higher things not because he had learned much Talmud and codifiers but because of the prayer he always prayed with great intention, and from there he merited a high level."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HasidismTefilaBeKavanah ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HasidismGiluiElyonim ;
    skos:note "The claim is double and the second half is the sharper: it names the cause and excludes a rival cause, learning, by name. It is reported as what the Rav's soul told him, which is the work's only report of a disclosure to a named person, and the speaker is a soul, so the claim attests what the circle said its master was told and not an experience in the first person."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 41, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sect. 41, p. 28" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 41" .

pmc:HasidismAliyaLemalaMehaRekiim a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Rising in thought above all the firmaments and speaking there"@en, "Im Denken über alle Himmel aufsteigen und dort sprechen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity of greatness, gadlut: to rise above all the firmaments, the thrones, the ofanim and the seraphim and to speak there in that world (136); to break through all the firmaments at once by thought and rise above the angels, which the work calls complete service (137)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 136-137, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 136-137, pp. 131-132" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 136-137" .

pmc:HasidismKatnutAliya a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Prayer in fear and love below brings the ascent above the firmaments"@en, "Gebet in Furcht und Liebe unten bringt den Aufstieg über die Himmel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Section 136: at times, even during prayer, one cannot rise and serves in fear and love below; by speaking in fear and love below and attaching oneself to the Creator, one will merit to rise above all the firmaments and speak there."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HasidismTefilaBeKavanah ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HasidismAliyaLemalaMehaRekiim ;
    skos:note "The work says as often that the ascent does not come. In section 135 there are many times when one can serve only in smallness and cannot rise to the upper world; in section 137 one may understand with the intellect that the world is as nothing before the infinite and still be unable to rise. No term, count or sign is given at which the smallness would count as a failure; it is a level and not a fault."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 135-137, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 135-137, pp. 131-132" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 135-137" .

pmp:HasidismNianua a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Swaying at the beginning of prayer"@en, "Das Wiegen zu Beginn des Gebets"@de ;
    skos:definition "Section 68: prayer is a coupling with the Shekhinah, and as there is motion at the beginning of coupling, so one must sway at the beginning of prayer; afterwards one can stand without swaying, attached to the Shekhinah in great attachment. Section 58 gives the same order for the body as a whole: one must first rouse oneself in the body with all one's strength, as the Zohar says a log that does not catch should be struck, and afterwards one can serve in thought alone without bodily motion."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sects. 58 and 68, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sects. 58 and 68, pp. 47 and 54" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sects. 58 and 68" .

pmc:HasidismHitlahavut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Hitlahavut, burning fervour in service"@en, "Hitlahawut, brennende Inbrunst im Dienst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fervour the work counts as total attachment (111). It says of it that it cannot be continuous but is reaching and not reaching, like a fire that the first blowing puts out and later blowing makes flare, going up and down, since continuous pleasure is no pleasure; and that what can be continuous is thought on the letters of the Torah, which are His garments."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 111, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sect. 111, pp. 101-102" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 111" .

pmc:HasidismNianuaHitlahavut a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TzavaatHaRivash ;
    rdfs:label "Swaying brings great arousal and fervour"@en, "Das Wiegen bringt große Erregung und Inbrunst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Section 68: from swaying one can come to a great arousal, for one will think, why am I swaying, surely because the Shekhinah stands before me, and from this one comes to great fervour."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HasidismNianua ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HasidismHitlahavut ;
    skos:note "The mechanism is stated, and it runs through an inference the swayer draws about his own motion: the body moves first and the thought then supplies a reason for the moving. It is the one place in the work where a bodily act is said to produce a state by way of the practitioner's interpretation of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Hasidism ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sect. 68, Hebrew text, Warsaw 1913" ;
    dcterms:source "Tzava'at Harivash, trans. Schochet (Kehot 1998), sect. 68, p. 54" ;
    dcterms:source "The Way of the Baal Shem Tov, trans. Markel and Markel (2020), sect. 68" .

